  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 1243 AM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nTuesday-Thursday...Despite the shortwave trough lifting into Canada, \nanother shortwave rotates into the base of the longwave trough over \nthe Northern Plains and Rockies, keeping the cold front propagating \ninto Iowa. Diffluent flow aloft and surface convergence will\nsupport scattered to numerous showers and storms (50-80% \ncoverage) along the front where the main axis of moisture will \nbe present. Severe threat with these storms should be limited \ndue to the displacement of the trough to the northwest. There is\nonly a 20-30% chance of SBCAPE exceeding 500 J/kg and at least \n25 knots of sfc-to-500 mb bulk shear per the 02.12z LREF run. By\nWednesday, the cold front is forecasted to clear most of the \nCWA with it stalling south of Highway 34. A new low pressure \nsystem near the Oklahoma-Kansas border could provide some low- \nlevel isentropic upglide and mid-level F-gen just north of this \nbaroclinic zone. PoPs will remain elevated on Wednesday around \n50-70%. The longwave trough finally reaches Upper Mississippi \nRiver Valley on Thursday, pulling the low pressure over the \nSouthern Plains northeastward through Illinois. PoPs (30-50%) \nshould be limited to along and east of the Mississippi River as \na high pressure sits over northern Iowa.\n\nFriday-Saturday...The high pressure over northern Iowa shifts over \nour CWA on Friday, likely keeping the area dry (70-90%). As \nnorthwesterly flow returns aloft, the door is open for quick \nshortwave trough passages which could bring bursts of precipitation \ninto next weekend.",
